// Collector intrinsics (§6.4.1).
//
// Memory is collected automatically and none of these is needed by ordinary
// code. They exist because two things the collector cannot see from the outside
// — a pointer that has escaped to C, and a pointer C will hold for longer than
// one call — have no other expression. All three return `void`: what they do is
// change what the collector may do next, not hand anything back.
//
// None of them is in the prelude. Reaching for one is a deliberate act, and it
// should read like one.

// Request a collection now. A hint, not a guarantee.
@public gc_collect :: #intrinsic("gc_collect") func () -> void

// A no-op that **counts as a use**, so `x` stays reachable up to this point. A
// value's live range ends at its last read, which is why a buffer handed to C by
// interior pointer can be moved or freed during the call unless this follows it.
@public gc_keep_alive :: #intrinsic("gc_keep_alive") func <T> (x: T) -> void

// Make `x`'s object immortal and immovable. That is a leak by construction, and
// it is the trade for handing a pointer to C for longer than one call.
@public gc_pin :: #intrinsic("gc_pin") func <T> (x: T) -> void
